Sval: Subprocess's kill() handles a process that exited a moment ago

BaseProcess.kill() checks exitCode, then sends the worker a kill without
handling its reply. The worker forgets a process once it has reaped it, a
moment before the main thread hears of the exit, so a kill in that window
rejects with "Invalid process ID" and nothing catches it. A caller cannot:
its promise is wait()'s. xpcshell fails a test file on that rejection. The
process has exited either way, and wait() reports it, so the rejection is
dropped. mozilla-central's kill() is unchanged; drop the patch once Mozilla
fixes it.

diff --git a/toolkit/modules/subprocess/subprocess_common.sys.mjs b/toolkit/modules/subprocess/subprocess_common.sys.mjs
index 819fd9159a..ff93d8346c 100644
--- a/toolkit/modules/subprocess/subprocess_common.sys.mjs
+++ b/toolkit/modules/subprocess/subprocess_common.sys.mjs
@@ -688,13 +688,16 @@ export class BaseProcess {
       return this.wait();
     }
 
+    // The worker forgets a process as soon as it has reaped it, before
+    // exitCode is set here, so a kill in that window finds no process. It has
+    // exited, and the returned promise reports that.
     let force = timeout <= 0;
-    this.worker.call("kill", [this.id, force]);
+    this.worker.call("kill", [this.id, force]).catch(() => {});
 
     if (!force) {
       lazy.setTimeout(() => {
         if (this.exitCode == null) {
-          this.worker.call("kill", [this.id, true]);
+          this.worker.call("kill", [this.id, true]).catch(() => {});
         }
       }, timeout);
     }
